      "closure_rationale": "Dynamic and generated fixed-policy loops have explicit result/effect/failure contracts. Rejection stops at the first excessive prefix; saturation continues required callbacks. Actual generated function definitions and O0/O3 symbols/disassembly separate policy specialization from retained indirect callback dispatch. The sum<=limit invariant justifies subtraction; each attempted acquisition receives exactly one total release, including empty/partially allocated failure. Projection borrows only until release. Output publishes only at whole-operation success, while callback effects remain. Empty/invalid admission and a term above limit are distinct. Actual Rust const-bool Result comparison is numeric only, not C callback ABI/ownership equivalence. Six compiler profiles pass4anchors and8compiled ordinary semantic controls including both retained pure reducer defects;4compiling learner stubs reject. Each profile matches18968 independent cases and93114 exact ordered callback events,9402 acquisition refusals,4718 projection failures and3546 range refusals, plus actual retained pure-sequence results. Real malloc/free and before/after-allocation cuts test partial ownership. Equal-policy repeated samples retain40000 calls of every callback and exact checksums; compiler helpers/stack/build observations remain source-bound. Required effectful transfer is implemented for caller projection, fallible acquisition and cleanup. Saturation at index0 does not omit later effects or final partial acquisition failure; failed results preserve99 while recorded effect prefixes survive. A modulo3 projection changes the numeric input before the checked fold. Dynamic/generated forms agree on status, output, order and resources, with explicit nonreentry/lifetime premises and13cost dimensions. No unaided delayed learner result is inferred. Bounded author support only."
    },
